      "settings": {
        "eyebrow": "The part you cannot see",
        "heading": "What 50kg/m³ actually means.",
        "body": "<p>Density is the weight of a cubic metre of foam. It is not the same thing as firmness, which is why a cheap bed can feel reassuringly solid in the shop and still be flat by March. Firmness is how it feels on day one. Density is how much foam is in there to keep feeling that way.</p><p>Most dog beds are filled with shredded offcuts or low-density foam, and they bottom out inside a year — at which point your dog is sleeping on the floor with a cover over it. Our orthopaedic cores are 50kg/m³, which is the reason the guarantee is on the foam rather than on the stitching.</p><p>This is how the orthopaedic beds are built. The fibre-filled nest beds are made differently, and we say so on the page rather than letting you find out in month three.</p>",
        "density_number": "50<sup>kg/m³</sup>",
        "density_caption": "High-density memory foam core, used across the orthopaedic range.",
        "scheme": "default",
